Date: Sat, 6 Mar 1993 20:56:16 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: BET: Living for another 50 years

Tim May: 
> (You know, the idea of some kind of "Life Expectancy Index," with
> updated numbers and _ranges_ of numbers... 
 
This is a great idea.  I suspect life insurance companies do detailed 
analysis along these lines, though based on past statistics rather than
the odds of future breakthroughs.  If we could start a large-scale 
ideas futures market  on the odds of future breakthroughs -- this would
require they be well-defined, which may be difficult -- the life 
insurers might become major participants in that market.  Alternatively
we might trade abstract "life expectency futures", which would open 
up the now-secret insurance data.  For the near-term, we can start with
the life insurance tables, if we can get anything more detailed than 
the ideal-weight charts, and add in our own assumptions.
 
Here's some interesting stuff I've found in recent research
forays:
   
* "Howard Fruitflies": Drosophila have been bred for lifespans 
double normal, and the investigator hopes to achieve 200%.  This 
is done by breeding for postponed puberty, but if the underlying 
mechanism can be found, the aging process might be amenable 
to genetic therapy.  "It's not just an increase in lifespan, but an
actual postponement of aging.  Their ability to reproduce and fly are
enchanced at old ages.  It would be like being 120 years old and 
still capable of playing a good game of tennis" (Michael Rose, 
evolutionary biologist at UC Irvine). [Science v250:622] 
    
A buzzword now making the rounds of demographers is 
"the expansion of mortality": increase in variance of the average 
age at death.  In other words, those who beat the odds, thru 
effort or luck, are beating them by wider margins. Also, life expectancy
at age 85 is increasing, even as mean life expectancy is levelling off.
The "Gompertz curve", the assumption that death rates double 
every 8 years after age 30, may turn into a different curve, 
with slower increases in the death rate after age 85.   If the  
Gompertz curve holds up, even large decreases in age-specific mortality 
rates (85%) increase mean life expectancy by only 50 years.  It will be
interesting  to track the population of centenarians over the next
two decades, to see how strong this trend away from the Gompertz 
curve is, and where the increase in variance trend goes.  (The 
population  involved right now is still too small to get really 
good statistics from our notoriously  innacurate sampling methods 
like the Census). [Science v250, 1990] 
     
The synthetic antioxidant propyl gallate increases the median
lifespan of banana fruitflies 120%, and their maximum lifespan by 45%.
[Gerontology 1992;38:192-195].  Sodium selenite slightly increases 
their median and maximum lifespan, by increasing catalase and glutathione 
peroxidase levels.  [Gerontology 1989;35(4):188-191].   
      
       
> >* Gene therapy to fix strategic DNA damage, increasing
> .....
> Maybe, though I've only heard of it being used in a very few cases.

It's only been allowed within the last four years, and every single
patient has to be approved by the NIH.  On the other hand, thousands
of different kinds of genes have been inserted into mice for thousands
of different purposes, curing dozens of mice-diseases just in the
last decade.  As far as I know it hasn't been tried for aging per se, 
partly because we don't know which specific genes get damaged, and 
partly because "natural" aging gets a very low priority in our deathist 
medical research community. 

It's actually quite easy to insert the genes; figuring out what 
they'll actually do is tougher.  This could be garage-level hacking 
in ten to twenty years, effectively removing state control over our 
genes.  Already DNA amplifying machines are being used by small
labs catering to medicine, detectives, etc. and can be had for
around $15K new.  In ten years, it should be similarly easy
to make and amplify viruses for gene therapy, except that statist
regulation will likely be severe.  Geno-anarchy!

Nick Szabo				szabo@techbook.com

