Since I'm probably the "you" mentioned here, I'll respond.

First, this discussion comes up every few months in some groups. Some folks
think it's paranoid to even think the NSA is reading these lists, others
think there's not even any debate about it. So, since I've never seen this
discussed here on this list, I'll go ahead and make some quick comments.

* A few years ago, NetNews was around 5 MB a day. I assume by now it's much
higher. Still, this is no strain to store on tapes, disks, whatever. (Sites
store days-weeks of NetNews articles, so the NSA could easily store many
years worth, on disks! Storage on DATs and custom archival tapes is of
course even more trivial.)

* Internet (and other networks as well, presumably) carries much more than
this, of course...all the ftp'ing, the chat lines, routine e-mail, etc. It
may be several hundred megabytes a day.

* the NSA has machines directly on the Internet ("DOCKMASTER" at the
National Computer Security Center, the NCSC, is the best known). There are
other ways to see most of the traffic.

* acres of fast computers and specialized machines exist at NSA
headquarters at Fort Meade, Maryland, and probably at other sites.
Bamford's "The Puzzle Palace" is required reading for interested folks.

* filtering text files is vastly easier than voice recognition (my lowly
Mac IIci can completely index--"On Location"--all of the text on a 128 MB
optical cartridge in less than 2 hours...NSA has machines that are 10,000
times faster). Thus, storing and filtering Net traffic for keywords or for
certain authors seems fairly easy.

* given that networks have been used for planning crimes, for communicating
with Mafiosos on the run (CompuServe dead drops), and for espionage, I'd
expect the FBI, NSA, DIA, and others to have a considerable interest in
monitoring traffic. Dissidents on the Net are another target (cf. the
latest flames between Serbs and Bosnian Muslims, between Turkish and
Armenian antagonists, etc.).

* while some think that keywords like "plutonium," "terrorism," "child
porn," and "Kevorkian" are used to trigger searches, I suspect the actual
filters are more complicated. Mere keywords (such as people use in their
.sigs to "confuse" the NSA computers) are not enough.

* for security clearances and whatnot, it seems likely that indexing
postings to controversial groups (alt.sex.children, rec.drugs.sellers,
etc.) will be done to allow a posting history to be "called up" at will.
Expect that _anything_ you've ever posted publicly to a UseNet group will
someday be available via ftp.

* I feel certain that the "Cypherpunks" list is being monitored closely. We
talk about too many "hot button" items for it not to be: crypto anarchy,
tools to make espionage easier, PGP, export of crypto, viruses, anonymity,
etc. 

We also have several well-known hackers: Phiber Optik (Legion of Doom),
Lloyd Blankenship (Steve Jackson Games case), John Draper (Cap'n Crunch),
Kelly Goen, Murdering Thug, Treason, etc. And several other well-known Net
figures are on the list, as well as some journalists. Finally, several
"*.army.mil" subscribers are getting the list, and have complained about
the content (especially on the use of polymorphic computer viruses),
pressuring Eric Hughes, the list maintainer, to somehow do something about
the content (he refused). 

(And we don't know who's getting the list and passing it on, perhaps
automatically, to others.)

* I suspect the Extropians list is occasionally looked at by NSA filter
programs and analysts, but that the content is deemed to be "harmlessly
crankish."

This is the reality as I see it today.

-Tim May


