Date: Sun, 7 Mar 93 02:49:02 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: IDEA FUTURES: Life Expectancy Index

Nick Szabo writes:

>Tim May: 
>> (You know, the idea of some kind of "Life Expectancy Index," with
>> updated numbers and _ranges_ of numbers... 
> 
>This is a great idea.  I suspect life insurance companies do detailed 
>analysis along these lines, though based on past statistics rather than
>the odds of future breakthroughs.  If we could start a large-scale 
>ideas futures market  on the odds of future breakthroughs -- this would
>require they be well-defined, which may be difficult -- the life 
>insurers might become major participants in that market.  Alternatively
>we might trade abstract "life expectency futures", which would open 
>up the now-secret insurance data.  For the near-term, we can start with
>the life insurance tables, if we can get anything more detailed than 
>the ideal-weight charts, and add in our own assumptions.

Glad you like the idea. And thanks for expanding on it.

This could also be a fairly well-defined instance of Robin Hanson's idea
futures ("i die futures"?), as we will be able to directly examine the
mortality rates. As one example, suppose the "accepted" odds of a
80-year-old are 77% to make it to his 81st birthday. If you think life
expectancy is increasing, you might bet that, in fact, 79% of 80 year-olds
will make it to 81. In other words, bets can be resolved in a year or so
(depends on accuracy of census data, hospital records, etc.).

The betting process will fine-tune the life expectancy estimates, as idea
futures are supposed ot do. (The exact statement of the wagers is still to
be worked out, of course.)

This is also something that could do several things:

* provide a test case for idea futures (with fairly straightforward
resolution via the mortality results for the year)

* fold in our Extropian ideas about vitamins, exercise, medical treatments,
etc., and also force certain claims made about this or that treatment or
trend to face reality (folks who *really* believe that cancer rates are
declining can put their money where their mouth is)

* be easy to understand....imagine a "clock"--like the famous "nuclear
clock" with the composite/best estimate of life expectancy, perhaps for a
25-year-old. This could even be published on the cover of each "Extropy" (a
shameless rip-off of the "Bulletin of Atomic Scientists")

* generate media attention

* also test anonymous bets and their effect on the process

Nick is right that insurers may even participate in the bet. With
anonyomous bets allowed, those with access to up-to-date info may place
"bets" to exploit their perceived side information. This will tend to
increase the accuracy of the estimates and provide liquidity.

(Ironically, I just met with Robin this evening, but didn't think to bring
this up.)

More ideas?


-Tim May




