david director friedman writes:
>The answer to that is that the court will not take account of my
>views in deciding whether my belief was reasonable, although they
>might in deciding whether I believe it. In a sense, this means that
>the legal system is not religiously neutral--whether I am the second
>coming of Christ is a religious issue, after all. On the other hand,
>here as in the case of schooling, I think the correct conclusion is
>not that they should be religiously neutral (think about the
>implications) but that these issues inevitably involve taking
>religious stands, and that that is an argument against having them
>done by the state.

I think my anarcho-capitalist leanings first became strong when I was
a very religious high-school student involved in an unusual christian
sect (some would call it a "cult"), and realized that the state was
taking sides on (what were to me) religious issues, and against my
convictions, in school and in many other contexts.  I was irate that
we were oppressed so, and saw the value of a state that stayed neutral
as far as possible for such controversies.

Of course I now don't hold most of those unusual convictions, but my
passion for protecting people with odd views remains very strong.
Don't underestimate the potential for alliances with folks with quite
different odd beliefs, and don't reject such alliances just because
you disagree with them strongly on many grounds.  What you many be
able to find wide and passionate support for it the position that
widely shared institutions, such as those run by the state, should if
possible not take sides on claims which some minority may take
passionate exception to.  And if you think about it there are very few
claims not like this.

I wonder, did similar experiences lead other folks to similar positions?

Robin Hanson

